<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuxtla Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sierra de los Tuxtlas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tuxtla Mountains, or Sierra de los Tuxtlas, are a volcanic range on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> coast in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Their basalt was quarried by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmecs</a> and hauled to San Lorenzo and other centres to carve colossal heads, table-thrones and other monuments. Tres Zapotes grew on their western edge and remained a regional centre long after the wider Olmec decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuxtla-mountains.jpg" alt="Tuxtla Mountains" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dongringo at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_martin1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
